description: Compact git commit message format (type(module): what + why)
alwaysApply: true
---

# Commit messages

Use a **single-line compact** subject:

```text
type(module): description
```

## type

One of: `feat` | `fix` | `docs` | `test` | `refactor` | `chore` | `ci` | `perf`

## module

Short scope for the area touched, e.g. `agents`, `runner`, `tool`, `tracing`, `openai`, `parity`, `vendor`, `ci`.

## description

Must state **what** changed and **why** (motivation / risk avoided), not only a file list.

- Good: `feat(runner): add max_turns enforcement to match Python DEFAULT_MAX_TURNS and fail closed on loops`
- Bad: `update run.rs` / `fix stuff` / `wip`

## Rules

- Prefer one logical change per commit; avoid bundling unrelated refactors.
- Do not use `--no-verify` or skip hooks unless the user explicitly asks.
- Body is optional; if used, keep it short and still lead with what+why in the subject.
- When the change introduces a known Python SDK divergence, mention the `D-NNN` id from `docs/DEVIATIONS.md` in the subject or body.
